{#- The head shared by every page a stranger can reach.

    These are the only pages a search engine ever sees, so the description and the social
    tags matter here and nowhere else in the product. One file because the alternative is
    four copies that drift, which is the lesson `_nav.html.j2` already records.

    Expects: `title`, `description`, and optionally `canonical`, `og_type` and
    `structured` — the last a mapping rendered as JSON-LD. A text page is a page about
    one book and says so in both: `og:type` was `website` on all four hundred of them,
    which is the value for the site's front door and not for a work, and there was no
    machine-readable statement of what the page is about at all. -#}
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
{%- if canonical %}
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical }}">
{%- endif %}
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ og_type | default('website') }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ title }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ description }}">
{%- if canonical %}
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical }}">
{%- endif %}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
{%- if structured %}
{#- What the page is about, said to a machine. Nothing here is a claim the page does not
    already make in its own text: the same title, the same byline, the same translator. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">{{ structured | tojson }}</script>
{%- endif %}
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
